WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.142
Film Courage: We are going&nbsp;
to talk about Rings of Power.

00:00:01.142 --> 00:00:03.480
Chris Gore, Founder/Owner of&nbsp;
FilmThreat/Author/Filmmaker: Oh no…here we go.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:10.320
Film Courage: What do you think about Amazon&nbsp;
shutting down reviews for Rings of Power?

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:17.940
Chris: Wow, Amazon shutting down reviews of&nbsp;
Rings of Power is very disingenuous. I am a fan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:24.420
of reviews on Amazon. I can't tell you how many&nbsp;
times it's helped me pick out the correct product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:33.960
and I'm talking not about movies here I'm talking&nbsp;
about just product reviews it's very bizarre to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:43.140
me also that even that being a critic is such&nbsp;
a weird thing like we we find critic reviews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:49.500
of things useful reviews of cars reviews of&nbsp;
restaurants these are all useful things right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:57.780
but when it comes to to movies television shows&nbsp;
that's where people's passions come out I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:05.820
that Amazon shutting down the reviews for Rings&nbsp;
of Power is well it's in their best interests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:14.340
but also exposes that they're not allowing&nbsp;
potential fans of the show to speak their minds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.200
I think it's a it's going to&nbsp;
be a mistake in the long run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:28.980
and I also think having seen the shows or at&nbsp;
least half of the first season at the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:35.640
of this taping it's really the whole thing&nbsp;
was just ill-conceived from the beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:43.380
and the fact that they really couldn't endear&nbsp;
true Tolkien fans in what they were attempting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:51.120
achieve is really disheartening but the shutting&nbsp;
down of those reviews is I mean they're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.260
show if they're not going to show up on Amazon&nbsp;
they're going to show up somewhere else they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:59.520
going to show up in a YouTube video they're going&nbsp;
to show up in a comment section on a YouTube video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.480
or something else they're going to people are&nbsp;
going to be able to speak their minds about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:08.580
how they feel about the show if it's not on the&nbsp;
Amazon website it's going to be somewhere else so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:16.140
there's really no way to to completely shut it&nbsp;
down and I think it shows that they're fearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:22.680
the the reviews of just regular people that want&nbsp;
to talk about their love or hate for the show.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.820
Film Courage: What is&nbsp;
pandering to film journalists?

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:32.400
Chris: Well look I could teach a class on&nbsp;
pandering to film journalists I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:40.440
why that this became a thing it kind of evolved&nbsp;
but you do see in a lot of you see this occur a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:46.680
lot when it comes to marketing when it comes to&nbsp;
marketing a film or a television show they'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:53.700
highlight certain aspects of the show that may not&nbsp;
be a big aspect of the movie itself but they'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.700 --> 00:03:00.540
throw in a quick line they call it sometimes&nbsp;
there'll be an inconsequential character who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:07.560
happens to be gay they call it queerbaiting right&nbsp;
so you'll get like a character who happens to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:16.080
gay who is in a TV show or a movie and then it's&nbsp;
loudly announced that this character is gay and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:23.700
look we have gay representation but the character&nbsp;
really is not meaningful to the overall story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:32.160
so I really hate it when I see that that Studios&nbsp;
Pander to journalists and what they seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:38.280
be looking for in the way in the interest of&nbsp;
getting like good press for a TV show or a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.560
and then I feel like it backfires because&nbsp;
it completely misrepresents the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:48.540
and TV show and then I do believe&nbsp;
that it can turn off the audience.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.540
Film Courage: How do actors bully fans?

00:03:52.160 --> 00:04:00.420
Chris: Oh I find it really sad to&nbsp;
see actors bullying fans these days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.920
I first of all I think it's completely unnecessary&nbsp;
and I think that if they've got time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:17.340
scroll through social media and bully fans over&nbsp;
opinions I think that's I don't know I don't think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.420
that's the best use of your time I think one of&nbsp;
the best things you can do for your mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:30.120
is just to not be on social media unfortunately&nbsp;
for me I'm on social media for for work for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:38.880
most part I don't really I don't use I don't use&nbsp;
social media as a way to fight with strangers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:46.680
and I look if I was running a studio and I hired&nbsp;
Talent the first thing I would do is have them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:51.840
pass over give me their passwords to all their&nbsp;
social media accounts they would no longer have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.940
access to them and I think kind of the less&nbsp;
I know about the personal life of a celebrity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:06.900
the more I tend to like them so whenever I see&nbsp;
like an unhinged actor or celebrity go after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:14.100
just someone on social media who might have rubbed&nbsp;
them the wrong way I just think that it's they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.720
they're they're usually having a bad day or&nbsp;
dealing with something the problem I do think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:26.040
with with actors is they go through stretches&nbsp;
where they work very hard and then the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:35.280
eight months of the year they have nothing to do&nbsp;
and that time that idle time can be spent unwisely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:43.020
so yeah I'm not a fan whenever I witness that I&nbsp;
think it's I think it's completely unnecessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:52.320
and it's just made me think less of the the&nbsp;
person in question I'll say also I do try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:58.920
when I see a movie that may have included someone&nbsp;
who was involved in some recent internet nonsense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:05.100
I try to separate myself from that so that&nbsp;
I can actually enjoy the work for what it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:13.620
in particular recently there was a case with&nbsp;
I was kind of reluctant to see Clerks three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:21.780
I just I've always enjoyed Kevin Smith as a&nbsp;
filmmaker in his early days I think he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:31.020
something of a hero to a lot of people with Clerks&nbsp;
one what he did is is amazing he made this movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:38.100
for $20,000-30,000 took it to Sundance and&nbsp;
sold it with the contract written out in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:44.760
napkin that's a true story and the fact that he&nbsp;
charged up credit cards and sold his comic book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:52.020
collection to get the money to make the film&nbsp;
is is amazing it's like a real life fairy tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:59.580
but I think Kevin on social media has involved&nbsp;
himself in conversations and fights that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:07.200
I just don't think he needs to what does he&nbsp;
need to prove it's like he's stalking his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:13.380
fans he doesn't need to do that so I so I was&nbsp;
kind of reluctant to see Clerks three I wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:18.540
to see it's the third in a series of films that&nbsp;
I've enjoyed with characters that I've enjoyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:25.080
and I saw Clerks three and he got me he got me I&nbsp;
mean there's things that are kind of cheesy about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:32.940
it I didn't love I think at some point Kevin&nbsp;
might want to outgrow the potty humor but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:41.700
movie Clerks 3 is a love letter to Indie Cinema&nbsp;
it's sort of his version of Cinema Paradiso in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:48.420
sense and it's the best performances ever out of&nbsp;
Brian O'Halloran and Jeff Anderson the two actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:57.540
that play Dante and Randall at the Kwik-E-Mart&nbsp;
like they're the they're the Clerks and you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:03.720
it's the best acting they've ever done in their&nbsp;
careers and he got me Kevin Smith got me I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:09.120
sorry to admit but it's sad because I think&nbsp;
that there is a huge audience of people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:16.920
used to be fans of Kevin Smith that have grown&nbsp;
to they've they've sort of moved away from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:24.600
work because of how he's presented himself on&nbsp;
social media I try to overlook those things and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:31.980
and I'm I'm a little disappointed that I think&nbsp;
people are going to miss out on Clerks three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:41.820
because of their feelings about the artist it's&nbsp;
tough to do sometimes it's really tough to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:49.620
sometimes to separate the real life person&nbsp;
from the work but if you can look past it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:56.700
for indiscretions on social media you could&nbsp;
you could benefit from that kind of thinking.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:01.320
Film Courage: In fairness to talent to&nbsp;
actors and filmmakers and screenwriters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:06.420
fans are just as guilty of&nbsp;
bullying. How do fans bully artists?

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:12.300
Chris: Well I think this gets down to you&nbsp;
that the fact that there are just so few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:17.220
Stars these days and I think a star is someone&nbsp;
who's on a pedestal but now we have access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:24.420
to everybody because everyone for the most&nbsp;
part or someone everyone's on social media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:29.160
stars are accessible I mean I can&nbsp;
talk to Chris Pratt if I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:39.360
I can talk to other actors who are on social&nbsp;
media and I think this has diminished diminished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:46.200
actors it's the there's really very few&nbsp;
people I would I would classify as a star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:49.620
I would say Brad Pitt Tom Cruise

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:59.220
maybe Tom Hanks but they're just very few real&nbsp;
stars today because they become so accessible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:08.460
and even their I tend to be someone that just&nbsp;
avoids gossip and for years I didn't even know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:14.820
that Nicole Kidman was married to Tom Cruise&nbsp;
I learned that later I feel like if it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:19.500
apply to a movie I'm going to see I don't&nbsp;
really care about a celebrity's personal life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:28.440
and I think ignoring it will make you enjoy&nbsp;
a movie much more but but it's disheartening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:38.100
that this tool we're still learning how to use&nbsp;
this tool and misuse this tool for a long time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:43.860
we didn't have seat belts and cars and for a long&nbsp;
time doctors recommended smoking for your health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:51.780
my doctor recommends this cigarette oh there's&nbsp;
print ads you can find them I think we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.780 --> 00:11:03.060
to find out I think we're going to find out that&nbsp;
social media is worse for us than smoking and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:12.240
some cases it's like holding a loaded weapon you&nbsp;
could ruin your career in a in a tweet so it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:20.940
unfortunate we're living in this time we're social&nbsp;
media and so-called influencers dominate so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:25.920
I just hope that we learn to&nbsp;
use this tool in a better way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:34.200
because it's really infected everything it's&nbsp;
affected entertainment I mean the fact that studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:42.420
marketing departments care what a small percentage&nbsp;
of people a Twitter mob thinks about a movie and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:49.080
will thus make changes in a movie think about&nbsp;
that they're making changes in a movie because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:57.360
a small percentage of people on social media care&nbsp;
about this thing when you could just as well say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:04.260
here's the work who cares don't go see it if&nbsp;
you think it's something you wouldn't like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:14.520
so I think being beholden to the Twitter mob is&nbsp;
just the worst thing someone who's creative can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:21.240
so do you think someone would see more star-like&nbsp;
if they're not accessible because then there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:27.420
like this mystery yeah I think we've lost&nbsp;
the mystery of what a star is or a celebrity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:35.160
we know too much about them including who&nbsp;
they voted for in the last election which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:42.960
again just again just sort of turns up the heat&nbsp;
on the rhetoric it's really disheartening to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:50.100
so if Marilyn Monroe or Rita Hayworth had been&nbsp;
on Twitter and we were able to see them too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:55.380
just walking to get coffee or I mean is there&nbsp;
something about the fact that we don't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.380 --> 00:13:02.160
know those Stars personal lives that well because&nbsp;
we found it in tell all books and things later on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:12.900
we got to know who's who was with who whom right&nbsp;
I just think it's I think it's social media has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:21.720
ruined a lot and in particular it's destroyed&nbsp;
the mystery of celebrities and stars and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:28.260
also now that it's like it does affect the&nbsp;
the content that's made it's effect affected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:32.160
movies and TV shows in ways that I don't&nbsp;
